{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:58:25.016Z","ordinal":67891,"type":"token_usage_record","payload":{"thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","session_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","root_turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","response_id":"resp_02692a5b008ee3bd016ac617af478887d0b24ce5879bed9196","usage":{"input_tokens":185389,"cached_input_tokens":181248,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":476,"reasoning_output_tokens":352,"total_tokens":185865},"turn_token_usage":{"input_tokens":31684491,"cached_input_tokens":31026944,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":150950,"reasoning_output_tokens":61188,"total_tokens":31835441},"thread_token_usage":{"input_tokens":1268194693,"cached_input_tokens":1242050432,"cache_write_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6289012,"reasoning_output_tokens":2811025,"total_tokens":1274483705}}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:58:25.030Z","ordinal":67892,"type":"event_msg","payload":{"type":"item_completed","thread_id":"01a1055e-4560-7b33-82d2-226827c76159","turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","item":{"type":"CommandExecution","id":"exec-5af85e47-2fa8-49be-8e95-a87c43c42378","process_id":"98435","command":["/bin/zsh","-lc","sed -n '1,195p' .agents/skills/package-structure/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,190p' .agents/skills/effect-client-wrapper/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,150p' docs/architecture/package-ownership.md && rg -n 'randomUUID|randomBytes' node_modules/effect/src/Crypto.ts && tail -9 /private/tmp/dev78-handover-whole-b-verification.log"],"cwd":"file:///Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit","parsed_cmd":[{"type":"unknown","cmd":"sed -n '1,195p' .agents/skills/package-structure/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,190p' .agents/skills/effect-client-wrapper/SKILL.md && sed -n '1,150p' docs/architecture/package-ownership.md && rg -n 'randomUUID|randomBytes' node_modules/effect/src/Crypto.ts && tail -9 /private/tmp/dev78-handover-whole-b-verification.log"}],"source":"unified_exec_startup","status":"completed","stdout":"---\nname: package-structure\ndescription: Scaffold, audit, migrate, or validate TypeScript packages with strict Effect v4 boundaries, explicit live-types exports, deterministic test Layers, and repository-local source conditions. Use for Effect service, RPC, or HTTP API packages; package export maps; live.layer.ts/test.layer.ts separation; package ownership; helper-sprawl prevention; or replacing stale package scaffolds. Do not use for repository-root or TanStack application scaffolding.\n---\n\n# Package Structure\n\nBuild the narrowest package that owns a real semantic boundary. Follow the\nrepository contract before the generic templates.\n\n## Resolve the repository contract\n\n1. Read the applicable `AGENTS.md`, root/package `README*`, architecture docs,\n   manifests, TypeScript/lint/test config, and current package exemplars.\n2. If invoked through a repository-local same-name skill, read its\n   `references/repository-profile.md` first. That profile owns package manager,\n   namespace, source condition, package exceptions, forbidden paths, and\n   package commands.\n3. Load the sibling [`docs-maintainer`](../docs-maintainer/SKILL.md) and its\n   repository-local profile when present. That profile—not the package\n   profile—owns documentation, runbook, proof, lifecycle, and archive routes.\n4. Inspect the worktree before writes. Preserve unrelated changes and do not\n   scaffold into a package until ownership is decided.\n5. Load [package contract](references/package-contract.md), then only the\n   relevant variant section in [variants](references/variants.md).\n\n## Select the variant\n\n- `effect-service`: domain/provider capability with Schemas, expected failures,\n  a contract-only service, production Layer, and deterministic test Layer.\n- `rpc`: RPC transport over an existing domain service. It owns RPC contracts,\n  handlers, clients, and transport—not domain policy.\n- `http-api`: HTTP transport over an existing domain service. It owns API\n  groups, status mapping, handlers, browser and host-supplied clients, and routes.\n- A transport client Layer can implement the existing domain Context.Service.\n  Keep generated native clients private and avoid a duplicate public contract.\n- Source-only or publishable packages are export/build policies applied after\n  choosing the semantic variant; they are not extra module trees.\n\nDo not create a package for one-use code, a pass-through wrapper, a generic\n`utils` bucket, or a boundary already owned by another package.\n\n## Render or audit\n\nFor a new package, use the canonical renderer. It refuses existing targets and\nhas no overwrite mode. It renders the compiled-internal policy only; source-only\nand publishable packages require a repository-specific audit/migration because\ntheir bundler and actual publisher own the proof:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/render_package.py \\\n  --kind effect-service \\\n  --target /absolute/new/package \\\n  --package-name @scope/catalog \\\n  --source-condition @scope/source \\\n  --versions /absolute/version-snapshot.json\n```\n\nRPC and HTTP API also require `--domain-package @scope/catalog`. Read\n[renderer and safety](references/renderer-and-safety.md) before invoking it.\n\nFor an existing package, run inspection first and make the smallest patch that\nbrings it to the owning repository contract:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/inspect_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\n```\n\n## Enforce the Effect boundary\n\n- `schemas.ts` owns Schemas, brands, and schema-derived types.\n- `errors.ts` owns expected failures. Serializable boundaries use the installed\n  Schema error factory (`Schema.TaggedError` in the qualified RC); internal-only\n  failures may use `Data.TaggedError`. Templates target the same qualified RC.\n- `service.ts` owns only `Context.Service` contracts—never a Layer or SDK.\n- `live.layer.ts` keeps host/SDK construction private and owns production\n  configuration/resources.\n- `test.layer.ts` creates deterministic substitution at the same contract and\n  may return `{ layer, observations }` scoped to one test.\n- Public methods use named `Effect.fn`; private Effect helpers normally use\n  `Effect.fnUntraced`. Use `Service.of` to check implementations and the\n  two-argument `Layer.effect` for effectful construction. Read the sibling\n  [service and Layer recipes](../strict-effect-ts/references/services/services-and-layers.md).\n- Decode unknown provider input/output once at ingress. Encode only at outward\n  HTTP/RPC/provider/persistence/report boundaries.\n- Keep operations lazy, flat, and sequential. Keep one-use mapping, decoding,\n  and error handling beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse,\n  independently testable policy, real I/O, or resource lifetime.\n- Reject generic SDK callbacks, raw client access, unbranded semantic strings,\n  primitive semantic config, runtime type-class branching, unchecked output,\n  scattered Effect execution, and speculative helpers.\n- When a public schema/service/transport changes, update the repository's\n  architecture owner and affected proof/critical journeys in the same slice.\n  Operational packages route to a repository runbook; pure packages state why\n  no runbook applies.\n\nRead [Effect service rules](references/effect-service.md) before changing an\nexternal client or live Layer.\n\n## Validate and hand off\n\nRun the repository's real format, lint, typecheck, focused tests, integration\ntests, build, packed-artifact, and downstream commands that apply. Also run:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_ecosystem.py /absolute/skill-or-repository-root\n```\n\nFor publishable packages, inspect the tarball produced by the repository's\nactual publisher; `publishConfig.exports` alone is not proof that npm or Bun\nremoved the development source condition.\n\nFor new or changed skill folders, run the global skill validator separately.\nRun the local docs-maintainer checks and attach the applicable impact rows and\nnon-claims. Report the selected variant, ownership decision, exports,\ndocumentation/proof impact, tests, commands, and any repository-specific exception. Render receipts\nretain official sources, qualified compatibility decisions, config digests,\nlimitations, and non-claims. Read [maintenance](references/maintenance.md)\nwhen updating templates, validators, or Effect APIs.\n\n## Strict data ownership in every package\n\nUse one Schema owner for each identity, constrained value and data record; infer\nits public types and reuse its fields and record-level checks in HTTP/RPC/provider\nand persistence codecs. Typed internal inputs/replies are already checked. Decode\nunknown data once at its real boundary, construct newly constrained values with\nthe owning fallible constructor, and encode only outward contracts. Meaningful\nIDs, URLs, dates, offsets and bounded text use owning brands rather than parallel\nprimitive definitions.\n\nDomain absence uses Option, including recursive source fields. Preserve old\nformats, missing/null distinctions, literal values and source hashes in codecs\nand compatibility tests. Use Effect Array/Record functions, immutable HashMap/\nHashSet and exhaustive Match throughout source, tests, tools and configuration.\nNo loops, native array traversal, reassignment or mutable/transient collection\nmode is justified by a readonly return type. Genuine shared state belongs in an\nowned Ref/transaction with a pure immutable update. Use readonly structured keys\nfor Effect caches and qualify sharing, full identity and cleanup on cancellation.\n\nSee the sibling strict-effect-ts Schema, collection and cache owners. The generated\npackage runs under its repository's actual strict lint configuration; the offline\nPython shape check is not proof of TypeScript policy, execution or cloud behaviour.\n---\nname: effect-client-wrapper\ndescription: Design, implement, or review a typed Effect service around a third-party SDK, API client, callback library, or Promise-based provider. Use for provider adapters, service Layers, external-client configuration, request and response Schemas, typed provider errors, retries, and test Layers. Enforce named operations, branded identifiers, Schema-backed Config, immediate output decoding, flat Effects, and the explicit rejection of generic use callbacks, raw clients, primitive config, instanceof, and unchecked SDK outputs.\n---\n\n# Effect Client Wrapper\n\nCreate a semantic adapter owned by the application, not a generic Effect-shaped escape hatch around an SDK.\n\n## Establish the boundary\n\n1. Read applicable repository instructions, architecture docs, lint rules, installed Effect and SDK versions, and representative services and Layers.\n2. Identify the owner of every request, response, identifier, configuration value, error, retry policy, and runtime boundary.\n3. Research the cloned Effect v4 source, record its revision/version, then reconcile the chosen APIs with installed source/types. Use matching primary SDK source for its contract; DeepWiki through Executor MCP is secondary explanation for the upstream Effect/SDK repository.\n4. Prefer extending an existing semantic adapter over creating a parallel client abstraction.\n\n## Define the public service\n\n- Expose named domain/provider operations such as `getCustomer`, `sendMessage`, or `renderImage`.\n- Accept owning Schema-derived inputs and branded identifiers. Never substitute raw `id: string`.\n- Return only decoded application-owned or explicitly provider-owned Schema types.\n- Keep the raw SDK instance private to the live Layer or adapter module.\n- Provide a live Layer and a mock/test Layer at the same service contract.\n\nDo not expose a generic `use`, `run`, `withClient`, callback, unconstrained generic result, or raw-client accessor. Those APIs erase operation ownership, output provenance, retry policy, and testability.\n\n## Implement each operation linearly\n\nUse the locally installed Effect APIs. A named operation should read as one flat program:\n\n1. encode the checked request at its actual outbound contract, without re-decoding internal typed data;\n2. call exactly one SDK operation through `Effect.tryPromise`, callback adaptation, or the repository's boundary primitive;\n3. map rejection immediately into an owner-named typed Schema error with safe diagnostics;\n4. decode the unknown SDK result immediately with the owning output Schema;\n5. apply operation-specific retry, a complete-operation deadline, tracing, metrics, and redaction policy;\n6. return the decoded value without another wrapper layer.\n\nUse `Effect.gen`, meaningful `Effect.fn`, or local equivalents for readable sequential work. Keep one-use request mapping, rejection mapping, and decoding beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse, stable domain policy, or a real I/O boundary.\n\nFor native Effect HTTP/RPC clients, reuse the domain service contract instead\nof exposing the generated client or inventing a second public service. Keep\nSchema-selected response checks and named transport/decode failures with their\nsemantic owner. Promise adaptation is unnecessary when the client already\nreturns Effect.\n\nA deadline includes headers and decoded body. Test both stalled phases, one\ntotal budget after late headers, underlying cleanup and earlier interruption.\nDo not reset the clock at each phase or add an automatic retry by default.\n\n## Configure safely\n\n- Model semantic configuration with Schema and `Config`/`ConfigProvider`, using the repository's supported API.\n- Give configuration an owner-named structure rather than passing independent primitive strings and numbers through the codebase.\n- Keep secrets redacted and unwrap them only for immediate SDK construction.\n- Construct the SDK once in the live Layer unless its documented lifecycle requires scoped acquisition and release.\n- Override configuration in tests through a test `ConfigProvider` or the repository's established Layer mechanism.\n\n## Model failures explicitly\n\n- Use owner-named Schema-tagged errors for expected configuration, transport, rate-limit, authentication, provider, and decode failures when those distinctions affect callers.\n- Preserve safe provider codes, operation names, retryability, and diagnostic context; do not place unchecked `unknown` payloads in public tagged errors.\n- Use tagged catches or pattern matching. Never use `instanceof` for provider-error policy.\n- Treat truly unexpected failures as defects only when callers cannot reasonably recover.\n\n## Adapt browser hosts and callbacks\n\nKeep the original host object when a method depends on `this`; do not detach\nthe method or decode it into a copied data object. Validate the required\ncapability with the owning Schema. Detect absence inside Effect and distinguish\nit from a supported host refusing an operation. Keep safe refusal details.\n\nScope callback execution and registration together. Release unregisters or\naborts registration and interrupts unfinished callbacks. Accommodate synchronous\nand Promise return forms only where a supported host requires them. Confine the\nbridge and justified lint exceptions to the named adapter. For a browser tool\ninterface, follow the sibling\n[MCP/WebMCP pattern](../strict-effect-ts/references/applications/mcp-and-webmcp.md).\n\nFor a PostHog browser SDK in a React-owned Effect Atom runtime, read the\n[PostHog browser adapter guide](references/posthog-browser.md). It covers scoped\nSDK ownership, explicit event operations, the synchronous capture callback,\nprivacy controls and real-client delivery proof. Native Distilled management\nand backend Effect HTTP capture do not need Promise bridges.\n\n## Test and enforce\n\nTest service operations through mock/test Layers and test the live adapter boundary narrowly. Cover success, request encoding, provider rejection mapping, output decode failure, redaction, timeout/retry policy, and non-idempotent retry exclusion.\n\nUpdate affected architecture docs, READMEs, lint/custom rules and fixtures, CI commands, SPEC/tasks, and repository-owned skills. Add the narrowest reliable static or stale-pattern check the repository supports.\n\nAcceptance requires zero examples or public APIs containing:\n\n- a generic SDK `use` callback or raw-client accessor;\n- a raw `id: string` where an owning branded ID exists;\n- primitive/manual semantic configuration in place of Schema-backed `Config`;\n- `instanceof` provider-error branching;\n- an SDK result escaping without immediate Schema decoding;\n- a one-use wrapper/helper that merely forwards, maps, reads one property, or hides the SDK call.\n\nRun the repository's actual format, lint, typecheck, test, build, boundary, skill-validation, and stale-pattern checks appropriate to the change. Do not invent commands or claim the wrapper is safe when any prohibited pattern remains.\n\n## Keep checked values checked\n\nThe live adapter owns actual untrusted replies. Decode there once and return the\nowning inferred type. A caller does not re-decode a typed service reply; a test\nLayer follows that same contract. Reuse the domain's branded fields and shared\nwhole-record checks in a provider codec, including checks lost when fields are\nspread. Keep expected identity, completeness and range checks as domain policy.\n\nNew branded values use the installed fallible constructor; map its issue to the\noperation's named error. Plain mappings from checked values stay immutable with\nEffect Array/Record functions. Optional provider/source fields decode to Option;\nhistorical missing/null distinctions belong in the source codec. Internal caches\nuse checked readonly keys directly, with equality, full-address and cancellation\ntests. Neither SDK typings nor another internal decoder replaces boundary proof.\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-07\nreview_trigger: package, app, root composition, or semantic ownership change\n---\n\n# Package ownership\n\nPackage ownership defines where new TaxKit code should belong as the repo\ngrows and which package will be allowed to define canonical contracts. It does\nnot imply that every named package exists today.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc routes ownership decisions. [Package boundaries](./package-boundaries.md)\ncontains the proposed package map and dependency direction.\n\nCurrent implemented code lives in:\n\n- `apps/api`\n- `apps/web`\n- `packages/core`\n- `packages/calculators`\n- `packages/content`\n- `packages/docs-content`\n- `packages/docs-examples`\n- `packages/docs-fumadocs`\n- `packages/api/http`\n- `packages/api/rpc`\n: Private compiled native Effect RPC transport. It owns separately versioned\n  calculator and documentation procedures, thin named service handlers, checked POST/JSON ingress and\n  caller-scoped private client Layers. Calculator/content Schemas remain with\n  their existing owners. Explicit test-only composition is separate; no app\n  runtime or backend fallback belongs here. T003 app-host composition is locally accepted; T009 exported tracing remains open.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n- `packages/rules/au/income-tax`\n- `packages/rules/au/pay`\n- `packages/rules/au/stsl`\n- `packages/scripts`\n- `packages/testing`\n- `packages/tsconfig`\n\nCurrent planned ownership placeholder:\n\n- `packages/ui`\n\nThe placeholder directories contain README guidance only. Do not route runtime\nimports, source ownership or build expectations to them until package manifests\nand source exports exist.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`packages/core`\n: Implemented shared primitives, fact descriptors, rule descriptors, graph\nmetadata, trace and ledger contracts, common tagged errors and calculation\nengine service. Public trace inputs and results are JSON values, and\n`SourceExtract.rowContract` names the extracted row contract without using the\nvague former `shape` field.\n\n`packages/domain/au/*`\n: Planned Australian date dimensions and domain facts that are not owned by a\nsingle rule pack.\n\n`packages/rules/au/*`\n: Official Australian rule packs, parameter tables, algorithms, source\nreferences, graph metadata, rule-owned calculator ids/context literals and\ngolden tests. Current implemented packages are `pay`, `income-tax` and `stsl`.\n\n`packages/testing`\n: Shared test helpers for workspace packages. It must not become a back door\nfor production-only runtime helpers.\n\n`packages/scripts`\n: Implemented private repository-automation package. It owns schema-backed,\n  Effect-native orchestration that crosses package owners, including the\n  release-readiness command, its command-runner service, live process layer,\n  deterministic test layer and runtime entrypoint. It invokes canonical root\n  and package commands without moving or duplicating their validator logic.\n  Constructor functions use `create*`; deprecated `make*` aliases remain only\n  for package compatibility.\n\n`tools/repository-paths`\n: Root-owned repository portability gate. It owns the closed machine-local path\n  policy, schema-backed safe findings, typed local process and file failures,\n  focused tests and Bun runtime entrypoint. It is not a reusable file-scanner\n  package. `@taxkit/scripts` may invoke its root command but must not mirror the\n  policy.\n\n`packages/api/http`\n: Implemented HTTP API package. It owns Effect HTTP API definitions, boundary\nschemas, thin server handlers, OpenAPI, typed HTTP clients and HTTP\nstatus/transport annotations. Its narrow `request-boundary` export also owns the\nshared streamed HTTP-body admission used by the RPC and native app hosts. The\nRPC package depends inward on that export and retains its compatibility alias;\nthe HTTP package has no RPC runtime dependency. Client and Layer constructors use `create*`;\ndeprecated `make*` aliases remain at the export owner for compatibility.\n\n`packages/calculators`\n: Implemented reusable calculator orchestration package. It owns calculator\ncatalog composition schemas, calculator service methods, metadata projections,\ngraph response construction, schema-guided error shaping and\nrule-pack/scenario composition used by HTTP, SDK, CLI and in-process callers.\nIt also owns the canonical reusable calculator run schemas named\n`CalculatorRun*` and the `CalculatorServiceError` union. HTTP-only public\nenvelopes and status annotations stay in `packages/api/http`; SDK schema\nexports may re-export calculator-owned run contracts but must not duplicate\nthem. Service interfaces use the precise `Contract` suffix rather than the\nvague `Shape` suffix. It depends on `packages/core` and rule packages, but it\nmust not depend on HTTP handlers, SDK clients, CLI commands or app runtime\nmodules.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n: Implemented private TypeScript SDK package for a future public SDK\npublication. It owns browser-safe schemas, typed calculation facades,\nEffect-native subpaths, jurisdiction modules, examples, compatibility tests and\npacked-artifact checks. Its strict downstream validator may orchestrate the\nnine-package release closure and materialize package-declared publication\nexports in temporary tarballs, but package manifests and exports remain owned\nby their packages. It must not depend on `@taxkit/api-http`; HTTP\ntransports call the calculator service directly; the SDK is a test-only\ncomparison dependency of the HTTP package. Its Effect entrypoint owns\nrequest-preserving calculator helpers such as `calculateRunRequest`,\n`calculateReportRequest` and `calculateReport`, while reusing\ncalculator-owned `CalculatorRun*` schemas and `CalculatorServiceError`.\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native TanStack Website candidate. It owns form presentation/commands, the\n  server runner and React Atom registry. The private server binding and checked\n  public browser address reach the separate API through native RPC. The root\n  restores encoded outcomes. Tax rules and calculations remain package/API-owned;\n  the three calculator pages and bounded T003 connection are locally qualified.\n  The same app owns checked documentation page loaders, app-specific MDX\n  composition, navigation/focus, search and responsive reading. Its server\n  returns checked backend discovery documents at their conventional addresses.\n  Both Markdown representations and generated share images are locally\n  qualified. Current release documentation checking selects this app.\n  The old `apps/docs` workspace and writer workflows are retired; its\n  verified original source and dated recovery records remain addressable.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Retirement tombstone only. The strict historical source bundle retains all\n  49 original files. The deployment runbook owns inspection and separately\n  approved recovery, rather than an active old workspace.\n\nRoot `alchemy.run.ts`\n: Static retirement record which the actual Alchemy importer refuses before\n59: *     randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n66: *   const bytes = yield* crypto.randomBytes(16)\n67: *   const uuidv4 = yield* crypto.randomUUIDv4\n95:  randomBytes(size: number): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n150:  readonly randomUUIDv4: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n155:  readonly randomUUIDv7: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n205: * and UUID generation from `impl.randomBytes`. Digest operations delegate to\n210: * `impl.randomBytes` must return cryptographically secure bytes of the\n220: *   randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n224: * await Effect.runPromise(testCrypto.randomBytes(4)) // => new Uint8Array([0, 0, 0, 0])\n232:    readonly randomBytes: (size: number) => Uint8Array\n239:  const randomBytesUnsafe = impl.randomBytes\n241:  const randomBytes: Crypto[\"randomBytes\"] = (size) => Effect.map(validateSize(\"randomBytes\", size), randomBytesUnsafe)\n247:  const nextDoubleUnsafe = (): number => readUint53(randomBytesUnsafe(7)) / 2 ** 53\n251:      const bytes = randomBytesUnsafe(7)\n264:    randomBytes,\n291:    randomUUIDv4: Effect.sync(() => Uuid.v4String(randomBytesUnsafe(16))),\n292:    randomUUIDv7: Effect.clockWith((clock) =>\n293:      Effect.succeed(Uuid.v7String(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(16)))\n296:      Effect.succeed(Ulid.ulidString(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(10)))\n   • Packages in scope: //\n   • Running //#test:quality-workflow:task in 1 package\n   • Remote caching disabled, using shared worktree cache\n\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: cache miss, executing e7830848b768980d\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: $ bun --bun vitest run --config tools/vitest.config.ts tools/quality-workflow\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n//:test:quality-workflow:task:  RUN  v5.0.3 /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n","stderr":"","aggregated_output":"---\nname: package-structure\ndescription: Scaffold, audit, migrate, or validate TypeScript packages with strict Effect v4 boundaries, explicit live-types exports, deterministic test Layers, and repository-local source conditions. Use for Effect service, RPC, or HTTP API packages; package export maps; live.layer.ts/test.layer.ts separation; package ownership; helper-sprawl prevention; or replacing stale package scaffolds. Do not use for repository-root or TanStack application scaffolding.\n---\n\n# Package Structure\n\nBuild the narrowest package that owns a real semantic boundary. Follow the\nrepository contract before the generic templates.\n\n## Resolve the repository contract\n\n1. Read the applicable `AGENTS.md`, root/package `README*`, architecture docs,\n   manifests, TypeScript/lint/test config, and current package exemplars.\n2. If invoked through a repository-local same-name skill, read its\n   `references/repository-profile.md` first. That profile owns package manager,\n   namespace, source condition, package exceptions, forbidden paths, and\n   package commands.\n3. Load the sibling [`docs-maintainer`](../docs-maintainer/SKILL.md) and its\n   repository-local profile when present. That profile—not the package\n   profile—owns documentation, runbook, proof, lifecycle, and archive routes.\n4. Inspect the worktree before writes. Preserve unrelated changes and do not\n   scaffold into a package until ownership is decided.\n5. Load [package contract](references/package-contract.md), then only the\n   relevant variant section in [variants](references/variants.md).\n\n## Select the variant\n\n- `effect-service`: domain/provider capability with Schemas, expected failures,\n  a contract-only service, production Layer, and deterministic test Layer.\n- `rpc`: RPC transport over an existing domain service. It owns RPC contracts,\n  handlers, clients, and transport—not domain policy.\n- `http-api`: HTTP transport over an existing domain service. It owns API\n  groups, status mapping, handlers, browser and host-supplied clients, and routes.\n- A transport client Layer can implement the existing domain Context.Service.\n  Keep generated native clients private and avoid a duplicate public contract.\n- Source-only or publishable packages are export/build policies applied after\n  choosing the semantic variant; they are not extra module trees.\n\nDo not create a package for one-use code, a pass-through wrapper, a generic\n`utils` bucket, or a boundary already owned by another package.\n\n## Render or audit\n\nFor a new package, use the canonical renderer. It refuses existing targets and\nhas no overwrite mode. It renders the compiled-internal policy only; source-only\nand publishable packages require a repository-specific audit/migration because\ntheir bundler and actual publisher own the proof:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/render_package.py \\\n  --kind effect-service \\\n  --target /absolute/new/package \\\n  --package-name @scope/catalog \\\n  --source-condition @scope/source \\\n  --versions /absolute/version-snapshot.json\n```\n\nRPC and HTTP API also require `--domain-package @scope/catalog`. Read\n[renderer and safety](references/renderer-and-safety.md) before invoking it.\n\nFor an existing package, run inspection first and make the smallest patch that\nbrings it to the owning repository contract:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/inspect_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\n```\n\n## Enforce the Effect boundary\n\n- `schemas.ts` owns Schemas, brands, and schema-derived types.\n- `errors.ts` owns expected failures. Serializable boundaries use the installed\n  Schema error factory (`Schema.TaggedError` in the qualified RC); internal-only\n  failures may use `Data.TaggedError`. Templates target the same qualified RC.\n- `service.ts` owns only `Context.Service` contracts—never a Layer or SDK.\n- `live.layer.ts` keeps host/SDK construction private and owns production\n  configuration/resources.\n- `test.layer.ts` creates deterministic substitution at the same contract and\n  may return `{ layer, observations }` scoped to one test.\n- Public methods use named `Effect.fn`; private Effect helpers normally use\n  `Effect.fnUntraced`. Use `Service.of` to check implementations and the\n  two-argument `Layer.effect` for effectful construction. Read the sibling\n  [service and Layer recipes](../strict-effect-ts/references/services/services-and-layers.md).\n- Decode unknown provider input/output once at ingress. Encode only at outward\n  HTTP/RPC/provider/persistence/report boundaries.\n- Keep operations lazy, flat, and sequential. Keep one-use mapping, decoding,\n  and error handling beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse,\n  independently testable policy, real I/O, or resource lifetime.\n- Reject generic SDK callbacks, raw client access, unbranded semantic strings,\n  primitive semantic config, runtime type-class branching, unchecked output,\n  scattered Effect execution, and speculative helpers.\n- When a public schema/service/transport changes, update the repository's\n  architecture owner and affected proof/critical journeys in the same slice.\n  Operational packages route to a repository runbook; pure packages state why\n  no runbook applies.\n\nRead [Effect service rules](references/effect-service.md) before changing an\nexternal client or live Layer.\n\n## Validate and hand off\n\nRun the repository's real format, lint, typecheck, focused tests, integration\ntests, build, packed-artifact, and downstream commands that apply. Also run:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_ecosystem.py /absolute/skill-or-repository-root\n```\n\nFor publishable packages, inspect the tarball produced by the repository's\nactual publisher; `publishConfig.exports` alone is not proof that npm or Bun\nremoved the development source condition.\n\nFor new or changed skill folders, run the global skill validator separately.\nRun the local docs-maintainer checks and attach the applicable impact rows and\nnon-claims. Report the selected variant, ownership decision, exports,\ndocumentation/proof impact, tests, commands, and any repository-specific exception. Render receipts\nretain official sources, qualified compatibility decisions, config digests,\nlimitations, and non-claims. Read [maintenance](references/maintenance.md)\nwhen updating templates, validators, or Effect APIs.\n\n## Strict data ownership in every package\n\nUse one Schema owner for each identity, constrained value and data record; infer\nits public types and reuse its fields and record-level checks in HTTP/RPC/provider\nand persistence codecs. Typed internal inputs/replies are already checked. Decode\nunknown data once at its real boundary, construct newly constrained values with\nthe owning fallible constructor, and encode only outward contracts. Meaningful\nIDs, URLs, dates, offsets and bounded text use owning brands rather than parallel\nprimitive definitions.\n\nDomain absence uses Option, including recursive source fields. Preserve old\nformats, missing/null distinctions, literal values and source hashes in codecs\nand compatibility tests. Use Effect Array/Record functions, immutable HashMap/\nHashSet and exhaustive Match throughout source, tests, tools and configuration.\nNo loops, native array traversal, reassignment or mutable/transient collection\nmode is justified by a readonly return type. Genuine shared state belongs in an\nowned Ref/transaction with a pure immutable update. Use readonly structured keys\nfor Effect caches and qualify sharing, full identity and cleanup on cancellation.\n\nSee the sibling strict-effect-ts Schema, collection and cache owners. The generated\npackage runs under its repository's actual strict lint configuration; the offline\nPython shape check is not proof of TypeScript policy, execution or cloud behaviour.\n---\nname: effect-client-wrapper\ndescription: Design, implement, or review a typed Effect service around a third-party SDK, API client, callback library, or Promise-based provider. Use for provider adapters, service Layers, external-client configuration, request and response Schemas, typed provider errors, retries, and test Layers. Enforce named operations, branded identifiers, Schema-backed Config, immediate output decoding, flat Effects, and the explicit rejection of generic use callbacks, raw clients, primitive config, instanceof, and unchecked SDK outputs.\n---\n\n# Effect Client Wrapper\n\nCreate a semantic adapter owned by the application, not a generic Effect-shaped escape hatch around an SDK.\n\n## Establish the boundary\n\n1. Read applicable repository instructions, architecture docs, lint rules, installed Effect and SDK versions, and representative services and Layers.\n2. Identify the owner of every request, response, identifier, configuration value, error, retry policy, and runtime boundary.\n3. Research the cloned Effect v4 source, record its revision/version, then reconcile the chosen APIs with installed source/types. Use matching primary SDK source for its contract; DeepWiki through Executor MCP is secondary explanation for the upstream Effect/SDK repository.\n4. Prefer extending an existing semantic adapter over creating a parallel client abstraction.\n\n## Define the public service\n\n- Expose named domain/provider operations such as `getCustomer`, `sendMessage`, or `renderImage`.\n- Accept owning Schema-derived inputs and branded identifiers. Never substitute raw `id: string`.\n- Return only decoded application-owned or explicitly provider-owned Schema types.\n- Keep the raw SDK instance private to the live Layer or adapter module.\n- Provide a live Layer and a mock/test Layer at the same service contract.\n\nDo not expose a generic `use`, `run`, `withClient`, callback, unconstrained generic result, or raw-client accessor. Those APIs erase operation ownership, output provenance, retry policy, and testability.\n\n## Implement each operation linearly\n\nUse the locally installed Effect APIs. A named operation should read as one flat program:\n\n1. encode the checked request at its actual outbound contract, without re-decoding internal typed data;\n2. call exactly one SDK operation through `Effect.tryPromise`, callback adaptation, or the repository's boundary primitive;\n3. map rejection immediately into an owner-named typed Schema error with safe diagnostics;\n4. decode the unknown SDK result immediately with the owning output Schema;\n5. apply operation-specific retry, a complete-operation deadline, tracing, metrics, and redaction policy;\n6. return the decoded value without another wrapper layer.\n\nUse `Effect.gen`, meaningful `Effect.fn`, or local equivalents for readable sequential work. Keep one-use request mapping, rejection mapping, and decoding beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse, stable domain policy, or a real I/O boundary.\n\nFor native Effect HTTP/RPC clients, reuse the domain service contract instead\nof exposing the generated client or inventing a second public service. Keep\nSchema-selected response checks and named transport/decode failures with their\nsemantic owner. Promise adaptation is unnecessary when the client already\nreturns Effect.\n\nA deadline includes headers and decoded body. Test both stalled phases, one\ntotal budget after late headers, underlying cleanup and earlier interruption.\nDo not reset the clock at each phase or add an automatic retry by default.\n\n## Configure safely\n\n- Model semantic configuration with Schema and `Config`/`ConfigProvider`, using the repository's supported API.\n- Give configuration an owner-named structure rather than passing independent primitive strings and numbers through the codebase.\n- Keep secrets redacted and unwrap them only for immediate SDK construction.\n- Construct the SDK once in the live Layer unless its documented lifecycle requires scoped acquisition and release.\n- Override configuration in tests through a test `ConfigProvider` or the repository's established Layer mechanism.\n\n## Model failures explicitly\n\n- Use owner-named Schema-tagged errors for expected configuration, transport, rate-limit, authentication, provider, and decode failures when those distinctions affect callers.\n- Preserve safe provider codes, operation names, retryability, and diagnostic context; do not place unchecked `unknown` payloads in public tagged errors.\n- Use tagged catches or pattern matching. Never use `instanceof` for provider-error policy.\n- Treat truly unexpected failures as defects only when callers cannot reasonably recover.\n\n## Adapt browser hosts and callbacks\n\nKeep the original host object when a method depends on `this`; do not detach\nthe method or decode it into a copied data object. Validate the required\ncapability with the owning Schema. Detect absence inside Effect and distinguish\nit from a supported host refusing an operation. Keep safe refusal details.\n\nScope callback execution and registration together. Release unregisters or\naborts registration and interrupts unfinished callbacks. Accommodate synchronous\nand Promise return forms only where a supported host requires them. Confine the\nbridge and justified lint exceptions to the named adapter. For a browser tool\ninterface, follow the sibling\n[MCP/WebMCP pattern](../strict-effect-ts/references/applications/mcp-and-webmcp.md).\n\nFor a PostHog browser SDK in a React-owned Effect Atom runtime, read the\n[PostHog browser adapter guide](references/posthog-browser.md). It covers scoped\nSDK ownership, explicit event operations, the synchronous capture callback,\nprivacy controls and real-client delivery proof. Native Distilled management\nand backend Effect HTTP capture do not need Promise bridges.\n\n## Test and enforce\n\nTest service operations through mock/test Layers and test the live adapter boundary narrowly. Cover success, request encoding, provider rejection mapping, output decode failure, redaction, timeout/retry policy, and non-idempotent retry exclusion.\n\nUpdate affected architecture docs, READMEs, lint/custom rules and fixtures, CI commands, SPEC/tasks, and repository-owned skills. Add the narrowest reliable static or stale-pattern check the repository supports.\n\nAcceptance requires zero examples or public APIs containing:\n\n- a generic SDK `use` callback or raw-client accessor;\n- a raw `id: string` where an owning branded ID exists;\n- primitive/manual semantic configuration in place of Schema-backed `Config`;\n- `instanceof` provider-error branching;\n- an SDK result escaping without immediate Schema decoding;\n- a one-use wrapper/helper that merely forwards, maps, reads one property, or hides the SDK call.\n\nRun the repository's actual format, lint, typecheck, test, build, boundary, skill-validation, and stale-pattern checks appropriate to the change. Do not invent commands or claim the wrapper is safe when any prohibited pattern remains.\n\n## Keep checked values checked\n\nThe live adapter owns actual untrusted replies. Decode there once and return the\nowning inferred type. A caller does not re-decode a typed service reply; a test\nLayer follows that same contract. Reuse the domain's branded fields and shared\nwhole-record checks in a provider codec, including checks lost when fields are\nspread. Keep expected identity, completeness and range checks as domain policy.\n\nNew branded values use the installed fallible constructor; map its issue to the\noperation's named error. Plain mappings from checked values stay immutable with\nEffect Array/Record functions. Optional provider/source fields decode to Option;\nhistorical missing/null distinctions belong in the source codec. Internal caches\nuse checked readonly keys directly, with equality, full-address and cancellation\ntests. Neither SDK typings nor another internal decoder replaces boundary proof.\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-07\nreview_trigger: package, app, root composition, or semantic ownership change\n---\n\n# Package ownership\n\nPackage ownership defines where new TaxKit code should belong as the repo\ngrows and which package will be allowed to define canonical contracts. It does\nnot imply that every named package exists today.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc routes ownership decisions. [Package boundaries](./package-boundaries.md)\ncontains the proposed package map and dependency direction.\n\nCurrent implemented code lives in:\n\n- `apps/api`\n- `apps/web`\n- `packages/core`\n- `packages/calculators`\n- `packages/content`\n- `packages/docs-content`\n- `packages/docs-examples`\n- `packages/docs-fumadocs`\n- `packages/api/http`\n- `packages/api/rpc`\n: Private compiled native Effect RPC transport. It owns separately versioned\n  calculator and documentation procedures, thin named service handlers, checked POST/JSON ingress and\n  caller-scoped private client Layers. Calculator/content Schemas remain with\n  their existing owners. Explicit test-only composition is separate; no app\n  runtime or backend fallback belongs here. T003 app-host composition is locally accepted; T009 exported tracing remains open.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n- `packages/rules/au/income-tax`\n- `packages/rules/au/pay`\n- `packages/rules/au/stsl`\n- `packages/scripts`\n- `packages/testing`\n- `packages/tsconfig`\n\nCurrent planned ownership placeholder:\n\n- `packages/ui`\n\nThe placeholder directories contain README guidance only. Do not route runtime\nimports, source ownership or build expectations to them until package manifests\nand source exports exist.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`packages/core`\n: Implemented shared primitives, fact descriptors, rule descriptors, graph\nmetadata, trace and ledger contracts, common tagged errors and calculation\nengine service. Public trace inputs and results are JSON values, and\n`SourceExtract.rowContract` names the extracted row contract without using the\nvague former `shape` field.\n\n`packages/domain/au/*`\n: Planned Australian date dimensions and domain facts that are not owned by a\nsingle rule pack.\n\n`packages/rules/au/*`\n: Official Australian rule packs, parameter tables, algorithms, source\nreferences, graph metadata, rule-owned calculator ids/context literals and\ngolden tests. Current implemented packages are `pay`, `income-tax` and `stsl`.\n\n`packages/testing`\n: Shared test helpers for workspace packages. It must not become a back door\nfor production-only runtime helpers.\n\n`packages/scripts`\n: Implemented private repository-automation package. It owns schema-backed,\n  Effect-native orchestration that crosses package owners, including the\n  release-readiness command, its command-runner service, live process layer,\n  deterministic test layer and runtime entrypoint. It invokes canonical root\n  and package commands without moving or duplicating their validator logic.\n  Constructor functions use `create*`; deprecated `make*` aliases remain only\n  for package compatibility.\n\n`tools/repository-paths`\n: Root-owned repository portability gate. It owns the closed machine-local path\n  policy, schema-backed safe findings, typed local process and file failures,\n  focused tests and Bun runtime entrypoint. It is not a reusable file-scanner\n  package. `@taxkit/scripts` may invoke its root command but must not mirror the\n  policy.\n\n`packages/api/http`\n: Implemented HTTP API package. It owns Effect HTTP API definitions, boundary\nschemas, thin server handlers, OpenAPI, typed HTTP clients and HTTP\nstatus/transport annotations. Its narrow `request-boundary` export also owns the\nshared streamed HTTP-body admission used by the RPC and native app hosts. The\nRPC package depends inward on that export and retains its compatibility alias;\nthe HTTP package has no RPC runtime dependency. Client and Layer constructors use `create*`;\ndeprecated `make*` aliases remain at the export owner for compatibility.\n\n`packages/calculators`\n: Implemented reusable calculator orchestration package. It owns calculator\ncatalog composition schemas, calculator service methods, metadata projections,\ngraph response construction, schema-guided error shaping and\nrule-pack/scenario composition used by HTTP, SDK, CLI and in-process callers.\nIt also owns the canonical reusable calculator run schemas named\n`CalculatorRun*` and the `CalculatorServiceError` union. HTTP-only public\nenvelopes and status annotations stay in `packages/api/http`; SDK schema\nexports may re-export calculator-owned run contracts but must not duplicate\nthem. Service interfaces use the precise `Contract` suffix rather than the\nvague `Shape` suffix. It depends on `packages/core` and rule packages, but it\nmust not depend on HTTP handlers, SDK clients, CLI commands or app runtime\nmodules.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n: Implemented private TypeScript SDK package for a future public SDK\npublication. It owns browser-safe schemas, typed calculation facades,\nEffect-native subpaths, jurisdiction modules, examples, compatibility tests and\npacked-artifact checks. Its strict downstream validator may orchestrate the\nnine-package release closure and materialize package-declared publication\nexports in temporary tarballs, but package manifests and exports remain owned\nby their packages. It must not depend on `@taxkit/api-http`; HTTP\ntransports call the calculator service directly; the SDK is a test-only\ncomparison dependency of the HTTP package. Its Effect entrypoint owns\nrequest-preserving calculator helpers such as `calculateRunRequest`,\n`calculateReportRequest` and `calculateReport`, while reusing\ncalculator-owned `CalculatorRun*` schemas and `CalculatorServiceError`.\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native TanStack Website candidate. It owns form presentation/commands, the\n  server runner and React Atom registry. The private server binding and checked\n  public browser address reach the separate API through native RPC. The root\n  restores encoded outcomes. Tax rules and calculations remain package/API-owned;\n  the three calculator pages and bounded T003 connection are locally qualified.\n  The same app owns checked documentation page loaders, app-specific MDX\n  composition, navigation/focus, search and responsive reading. Its server\n  returns checked backend discovery documents at their conventional addresses.\n  Both Markdown representations and generated share images are locally\n  qualified. Current release documentation checking selects this app.\n  The old `apps/docs` workspace and writer workflows are retired; its\n  verified original source and dated recovery records remain addressable.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Retirement tombstone only. The strict historical source bundle retains all\n  49 original files. The deployment runbook owns inspection and separately\n  approved recovery, rather than an active old workspace.\n\nRoot `alchemy.run.ts`\n: Static retirement record which the actual Alchemy importer refuses before\n59: *     randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n66: *   const bytes = yield* crypto.randomBytes(16)\n67: *   const uuidv4 = yield* crypto.randomUUIDv4\n95:  randomBytes(size: number): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n150:  readonly randomUUIDv4: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n155:  readonly randomUUIDv7: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n205: * and UUID generation from `impl.randomBytes`. Digest operations delegate to\n210: * `impl.randomBytes` must return cryptographically secure bytes of the\n220: *   randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n224: * await Effect.runPromise(testCrypto.randomBytes(4)) // => new Uint8Array([0, 0, 0, 0])\n232:    readonly randomBytes: (size: number) => Uint8Array\n239:  const randomBytesUnsafe = impl.randomBytes\n241:  const randomBytes: Crypto[\"randomBytes\"] = (size) => Effect.map(validateSize(\"randomBytes\", size), randomBytesUnsafe)\n247:  const nextDoubleUnsafe = (): number => readUint53(randomBytesUnsafe(7)) / 2 ** 53\n251:      const bytes = randomBytesUnsafe(7)\n264:    randomBytes,\n291:    randomUUIDv4: Effect.sync(() => Uuid.v4String(randomBytesUnsafe(16))),\n292:    randomUUIDv7: Effect.clockWith((clock) =>\n293:      Effect.succeed(Uuid.v7String(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(16)))\n296:      Effect.succeed(Ulid.ulidString(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(10)))\n   • Packages in scope: //\n   • Running //#test:quality-workflow:task in 1 package\n   • Remote caching disabled, using shared worktree cache\n\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: cache miss, executing e7830848b768980d\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: $ bun --bun vitest run --config tools/vitest.config.ts tools/quality-workflow\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n//:test:quality-workflow:task:  RUN  v5.0.3 /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n","exit_code":0,"duration":{"secs":0,"nanos":5541},"formatted_output":"---\nname: package-structure\ndescription: Scaffold, audit, migrate, or validate TypeScript packages with strict Effect v4 boundaries, explicit live-types exports, deterministic test Layers, and repository-local source conditions. Use for Effect service, RPC, or HTTP API packages; package export maps; live.layer.ts/test.layer.ts separation; package ownership; helper-sprawl prevention; or replacing stale package scaffolds. Do not use for repository-root or TanStack application scaffolding.\n---\n\n# Package Structure\n\nBuild the narrowest package that owns a real semantic boundary. Follow the\nrepository contract before the generic templates.\n\n## Resolve the repository contract\n\n1. Read the applicable `AGENTS.md`, root/package `README*`, architecture docs,\n   manifests, TypeScript/lint/test config, and current package exemplars.\n2. If invoked through a repository-local same-name skill, read its\n   `references/repository-profile.md` first. That profile owns package manager,\n   namespace, source condition, package exceptions, forbidden paths, and\n   package commands.\n3. Load the sibling [`docs-maintainer`](../docs-maintainer/SKILL.md) and its\n   repository-local profile when present. That profile—not the package\n   profile—owns documentation, runbook, proof, lifecycle, and archive routes.\n4. Inspect the worktree before writes. Preserve unrelated changes and do not\n   scaffold into a package until ownership is decided.\n5. Load [package contract](references/package-contract.md), then only the\n   relevant variant section in [variants](references/variants.md).\n\n## Select the variant\n\n- `effect-service`: domain/provider capability with Schemas, expected failures,\n  a contract-only service, production Layer, and deterministic test Layer.\n- `rpc`: RPC transport over an existing domain service. It owns RPC contracts,\n  handlers, clients, and transport—not domain policy.\n- `http-api`: HTTP transport over an existing domain service. It owns API\n  groups, status mapping, handlers, browser and host-supplied clients, and routes.\n- A transport client Layer can implement the existing domain Context.Service.\n  Keep generated native clients private and avoid a duplicate public contract.\n- Source-only or publishable packages are export/build policies applied after\n  choosing the semantic variant; they are not extra module trees.\n\nDo not create a package for one-use code, a pass-through wrapper, a generic\n`utils` bucket, or a boundary already owned by another package.\n\n## Render or audit\n\nFor a new package, use the canonical renderer. It refuses existing targets and\nhas no overwrite mode. It renders the compiled-internal policy only; source-only\nand publishable packages require a repository-specific audit/migration because\ntheir bundler and actual publisher own the proof:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/render_package.py \\\n  --kind effect-service \\\n  --target /absolute/new/package \\\n  --package-name @scope/catalog \\\n  --source-condition @scope/source \\\n  --versions /absolute/version-snapshot.json\n```\n\nRPC and HTTP API also require `--domain-package @scope/catalog`. Read\n[renderer and safety](references/renderer-and-safety.md) before invoking it.\n\nFor an existing package, run inspection first and make the smallest patch that\nbrings it to the owning repository contract:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/inspect_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\n```\n\n## Enforce the Effect boundary\n\n- `schemas.ts` owns Schemas, brands, and schema-derived types.\n- `errors.ts` owns expected failures. Serializable boundaries use the installed\n  Schema error factory (`Schema.TaggedError` in the qualified RC); internal-only\n  failures may use `Data.TaggedError`. Templates target the same qualified RC.\n- `service.ts` owns only `Context.Service` contracts—never a Layer or SDK.\n- `live.layer.ts` keeps host/SDK construction private and owns production\n  configuration/resources.\n- `test.layer.ts` creates deterministic substitution at the same contract and\n  may return `{ layer, observations }` scoped to one test.\n- Public methods use named `Effect.fn`; private Effect helpers normally use\n  `Effect.fnUntraced`. Use `Service.of` to check implementations and the\n  two-argument `Layer.effect` for effectful construction. Read the sibling\n  [service and Layer recipes](../strict-effect-ts/references/services/services-and-layers.md).\n- Decode unknown provider input/output once at ingress. Encode only at outward\n  HTTP/RPC/provider/persistence/report boundaries.\n- Keep operations lazy, flat, and sequential. Keep one-use mapping, decoding,\n  and error handling beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse,\n  independently testable policy, real I/O, or resource lifetime.\n- Reject generic SDK callbacks, raw client access, unbranded semantic strings,\n  primitive semantic config, runtime type-class branching, unchecked output,\n  scattered Effect execution, and speculative helpers.\n- When a public schema/service/transport changes, update the repository's\n  architecture owner and affected proof/critical journeys in the same slice.\n  Operational packages route to a repository runbook; pure packages state why\n  no runbook applies.\n\nRead [Effect service rules](references/effect-service.md) before changing an\nexternal client or live Layer.\n\n## Validate and hand off\n\nRun the repository's real format, lint, typecheck, focused tests, integration\ntests, build, packed-artifact, and downstream commands that apply. Also run:\n\n```bash\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\npython3 scripts/validate_ecosystem.py /absolute/skill-or-repository-root\n```\n\nFor publishable packages, inspect the tarball produced by the repository's\nactual publisher; `publishConfig.exports` alone is not proof that npm or Bun\nremoved the development source condition.\n\nFor new or changed skill folders, run the global skill validator separately.\nRun the local docs-maintainer checks and attach the applicable impact rows and\nnon-claims. Report the selected variant, ownership decision, exports,\ndocumentation/proof impact, tests, commands, and any repository-specific exception. Render receipts\nretain official sources, qualified compatibility decisions, config digests,\nlimitations, and non-claims. Read [maintenance](references/maintenance.md)\nwhen updating templates, validators, or Effect APIs.\n\n## Strict data ownership in every package\n\nUse one Schema owner for each identity, constrained value and data record; infer\nits public types and reuse its fields and record-level checks in HTTP/RPC/provider\nand persistence codecs. Typed internal inputs/replies are already checked. Decode\nunknown data once at its real boundary, construct newly constrained values with\nthe owning fallible constructor, and encode only outward contracts. Meaningful\nIDs, URLs, dates, offsets and bounded text use owning brands rather than parallel\nprimitive definitions.\n\nDomain absence uses Option, including recursive source fields. Preserve old\nformats, missing/null distinctions, literal values and source hashes in codecs\nand compatibility tests. Use Effect Array/Record functions, immutable HashMap/\nHashSet and exhaustive Match throughout source, tests, tools and configuration.\nNo loops, native array traversal, reassignment or mutable/transient collection\nmode is justified by a readonly return type. Genuine shared state belongs in an\nowned Ref/transaction with a pure immutable update. Use readonly structured keys\nfor Effect caches and qualify sharing, full identity and cleanup on cancellation.\n\nSee the sibling strict-effect-ts Schema, collection and cache owners. The generated\npackage runs under its repository's actual strict lint configuration; the offline\nPython shape check is not proof of TypeScript policy, execution or cloud behaviour.\n---\nname: effect-client-wrapper\ndescription: Design, implement, or review a typed Effect service around a third-party SDK, API client, callback library, or Promise-based provider. Use for provider adapters, service Layers, external-client configuration, request and response Schemas, typed provider errors, retries, and test Layers. Enforce named operations, branded identifiers, Schema-backed Config, immediate output decoding, flat Effects, and the explicit rejection of generic use callbacks, raw clients, primitive config, instanceof, and unchecked SDK outputs.\n---\n\n# Effect Client Wrapper\n\nCreate a semantic adapter owned by the application, not a generic Effect-shaped escape hatch around an SDK.\n\n## Establish the boundary\n\n1. Read applicable repository instructions, architecture docs, lint rules, installed Effect and SDK versions, and representative services and Layers.\n2. Identify the owner of every request, response, identifier, configuration value, error, retry policy, and runtime boundary.\n3. Research the cloned Effect v4 source, record its revision/version, then reconcile the chosen APIs with installed source/types. Use matching primary SDK source for its contract; DeepWiki through Executor MCP is secondary explanation for the upstream Effect/SDK repository.\n4. Prefer extending an existing semantic adapter over creating a parallel client abstraction.\n\n## Define the public service\n\n- Expose named domain/provider operations such as `getCustomer`, `sendMessage`, or `renderImage`.\n- Accept owning Schema-derived inputs and branded identifiers. Never substitute raw `id: string`.\n- Return only decoded application-owned or explicitly provider-owned Schema types.\n- Keep the raw SDK instance private to the live Layer or adapter module.\n- Provide a live Layer and a mock/test Layer at the same service contract.\n\nDo not expose a generic `use`, `run`, `withClient`, callback, unconstrained generic result, or raw-client accessor. Those APIs erase operation ownership, output provenance, retry policy, and testability.\n\n## Implement each operation linearly\n\nUse the locally installed Effect APIs. A named operation should read as one flat program:\n\n1. encode the checked request at its actual outbound contract, without re-decoding internal typed data;\n2. call exactly one SDK operation through `Effect.tryPromise`, callback adaptation, or the repository's boundary primitive;\n3. map rejection immediately into an owner-named typed Schema error with safe diagnostics;\n4. decode the unknown SDK result immediately with the owning output Schema;\n5. apply operation-specific retry, a complete-operation deadline, tracing, metrics, and redaction policy;\n6. return the decoded value without another wrapper layer.\n\nUse `Effect.gen`, meaningful `Effect.fn`, or local equivalents for readable sequential work. Keep one-use request mapping, rejection mapping, and decoding beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse, stable domain policy, or a real I/O boundary.\n\nFor native Effect HTTP/RPC clients, reuse the domain service contract instead\nof exposing the generated client or inventing a second public service. Keep\nSchema-selected response checks and named transport/decode failures with their\nsemantic owner. Promise adaptation is unnecessary when the client already\nreturns Effect.\n\nA deadline includes headers and decoded body. Test both stalled phases, one\ntotal budget after late headers, underlying cleanup and earlier interruption.\nDo not reset the clock at each phase or add an automatic retry by default.\n\n## Configure safely\n\n- Model semantic configuration with Schema and `Config`/`ConfigProvider`, using the repository's supported API.\n- Give configuration an owner-named structure rather than passing independent primitive strings and numbers through the codebase.\n- Keep secrets redacted and unwrap them only for immediate SDK construction.\n- Construct the SDK once in the live Layer unless its documented lifecycle requires scoped acquisition and release.\n- Override configuration in tests through a test `ConfigProvider` or the repository's established Layer mechanism.\n\n## Model failures explicitly\n\n- Use owner-named Schema-tagged errors for expected configuration, transport, rate-limit, authentication, provider, and decode failures when those distinctions affect callers.\n- Preserve safe provider codes, operation names, retryability, and diagnostic context; do not place unchecked `unknown` payloads in public tagged errors.\n- Use tagged catches or pattern matching. Never use `instanceof` for provider-error policy.\n- Treat truly unexpected failures as defects only when callers cannot reasonably recover.\n\n## Adapt browser hosts and callbacks\n\nKeep the original host object when a method depends on `this`; do not detach\nthe method or decode it into a copied data object. Validate the required\ncapability with the owning Schema. Detect absence inside Effect and distinguish\nit from a supported host refusing an operation. Keep safe refusal details.\n\nScope callback execution and registration together. Release unregisters or\naborts registration and interrupts unfinished callbacks. Accommodate synchronous\nand Promise return forms only where a supported host requires them. Confine the\nbridge and justified lint exceptions to the named adapter. For a browser tool\ninterface, follow the sibling\n[MCP/WebMCP pattern](../strict-effect-ts/references/applications/mcp-and-webmcp.md).\n\nFor a PostHog browser SDK in a React-owned Effect Atom runtime, read the\n[PostHog browser adapter guide](references/posthog-browser.md). It covers scoped\nSDK ownership, explicit event operations, the synchronous capture callback,\nprivacy controls and real-client delivery proof. Native Distilled management\nand backend Effect HTTP capture do not need Promise bridges.\n\n## Test and enforce\n\nTest service operations through mock/test Layers and test the live adapter boundary narrowly. Cover success, request encoding, provider rejection mapping, output decode failure, redaction, timeout/retry policy, and non-idempotent retry exclusion.\n\nUpdate affected architecture docs, READMEs, lint/custom rules and fixtures, CI commands, SPEC/tasks, and repository-owned skills. Add the narrowest reliable static or stale-pattern check the repository supports.\n\nAcceptance requires zero examples or public APIs containing:\n\n- a generic SDK `use` callback or raw-client accessor;\n- a raw `id: string` where an owning branded ID exists;\n- primitive/manual semantic configuration in place of Schema-backed `Config`;\n- `instanceof` provider-error branching;\n- an SDK result escaping without immediate Schema decoding;\n- a one-use wrapper/helper that merely forwards, maps, reads one property, or hides the SDK call.\n\nRun the repository's actual format, lint, typecheck, test, build, boundary, skill-validation, and stale-pattern checks appropriate to the change. Do not invent commands or claim the wrapper is safe when any prohibited pattern remains.\n\n## Keep checked values checked\n\nThe live adapter owns actual untrusted replies. Decode there once and return the\nowning inferred type. A caller does not re-decode a typed service reply; a test\nLayer follows that same contract. Reuse the domain's branded fields and shared\nwhole-record checks in a provider codec, including checks lost when fields are\nspread. Keep expected identity, completeness and range checks as domain policy.\n\nNew branded values use the installed fallible constructor; map its issue to the\noperation's named error. Plain mappings from checked values stay immutable with\nEffect Array/Record functions. Optional provider/source fields decode to Option;\nhistorical missing/null distinctions belong in the source codec. Internal caches\nuse checked readonly keys directly, with equality, full-address and cancellation\ntests. Neither SDK typings nor another internal decoder replaces boundary proof.\n---\ndocument_type: architecture\nlifecycle: current\nauthority: canonical\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-07\nreview_trigger: package, app, root composition, or semantic ownership change\n---\n\n# Package ownership\n\nPackage ownership defines where new TaxKit code should belong as the repo\ngrows and which package will be allowed to define canonical contracts. It does\nnot imply that every named package exists today.\n\n## Scope\n\nThis doc routes ownership decisions. [Package boundaries](./package-boundaries.md)\ncontains the proposed package map and dependency direction.\n\nCurrent implemented code lives in:\n\n- `apps/api`\n- `apps/web`\n- `packages/core`\n- `packages/calculators`\n- `packages/content`\n- `packages/docs-content`\n- `packages/docs-examples`\n- `packages/docs-fumadocs`\n- `packages/api/http`\n- `packages/api/rpc`\n: Private compiled native Effect RPC transport. It owns separately versioned\n  calculator and documentation procedures, thin named service handlers, checked POST/JSON ingress and\n  caller-scoped private client Layers. Calculator/content Schemas remain with\n  their existing owners. Explicit test-only composition is separate; no app\n  runtime or backend fallback belongs here. T003 app-host composition is locally accepted; T009 exported tracing remains open.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n- `packages/rules/au/income-tax`\n- `packages/rules/au/pay`\n- `packages/rules/au/stsl`\n- `packages/scripts`\n- `packages/testing`\n- `packages/tsconfig`\n\nCurrent planned ownership placeholder:\n\n- `packages/ui`\n\nThe placeholder directories contain README guidance only. Do not route runtime\nimports, source ownership or build expectations to them until package manifests\nand source exports exist.\n\n## Main areas\n\n`packages/core`\n: Implemented shared primitives, fact descriptors, rule descriptors, graph\nmetadata, trace and ledger contracts, common tagged errors and calculation\nengine service. Public trace inputs and results are JSON values, and\n`SourceExtract.rowContract` names the extracted row contract without using the\nvague former `shape` field.\n\n`packages/domain/au/*`\n: Planned Australian date dimensions and domain facts that are not owned by a\nsingle rule pack.\n\n`packages/rules/au/*`\n: Official Australian rule packs, parameter tables, algorithms, source\nreferences, graph metadata, rule-owned calculator ids/context literals and\ngolden tests. Current implemented packages are `pay`, `income-tax` and `stsl`.\n\n`packages/testing`\n: Shared test helpers for workspace packages. It must not become a back door\nfor production-only runtime helpers.\n\n`packages/scripts`\n: Implemented private repository-automation package. It owns schema-backed,\n  Effect-native orchestration that crosses package owners, including the\n  release-readiness command, its command-runner service, live process layer,\n  deterministic test layer and runtime entrypoint. It invokes canonical root\n  and package commands without moving or duplicating their validator logic.\n  Constructor functions use `create*`; deprecated `make*` aliases remain only\n  for package compatibility.\n\n`tools/repository-paths`\n: Root-owned repository portability gate. It owns the closed machine-local path\n  policy, schema-backed safe findings, typed local process and file failures,\n  focused tests and Bun runtime entrypoint. It is not a reusable file-scanner\n  package. `@taxkit/scripts` may invoke its root command but must not mirror the\n  policy.\n\n`packages/api/http`\n: Implemented HTTP API package. It owns Effect HTTP API definitions, boundary\nschemas, thin server handlers, OpenAPI, typed HTTP clients and HTTP\nstatus/transport annotations. Its narrow `request-boundary` export also owns the\nshared streamed HTTP-body admission used by the RPC and native app hosts. The\nRPC package depends inward on that export and retains its compatibility alias;\nthe HTTP package has no RPC runtime dependency. Client and Layer constructors use `create*`;\ndeprecated `make*` aliases remain at the export owner for compatibility.\n\n`packages/calculators`\n: Implemented reusable calculator orchestration package. It owns calculator\ncatalog composition schemas, calculator service methods, metadata projections,\ngraph response construction, schema-guided error shaping and\nrule-pack/scenario composition used by HTTP, SDK, CLI and in-process callers.\nIt also owns the canonical reusable calculator run schemas named\n`CalculatorRun*` and the `CalculatorServiceError` union. HTTP-only public\nenvelopes and status annotations stay in `packages/api/http`; SDK schema\nexports may re-export calculator-owned run contracts but must not duplicate\nthem. Service interfaces use the precise `Contract` suffix rather than the\nvague `Shape` suffix. It depends on `packages/core` and rule packages, but it\nmust not depend on HTTP handlers, SDK clients, CLI commands or app runtime\nmodules.\n\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\n: Implemented private TypeScript SDK package for a future public SDK\npublication. It owns browser-safe schemas, typed calculation facades,\nEffect-native subpaths, jurisdiction modules, examples, compatibility tests and\npacked-artifact checks. Its strict downstream validator may orchestrate the\nnine-package release closure and materialize package-declared publication\nexports in temporary tarballs, but package manifests and exports remain owned\nby their packages. It must not depend on `@taxkit/api-http`; HTTP\ntransports call the calculator service directly; the SDK is a test-only\ncomparison dependency of the HTTP package. Its Effect entrypoint owns\nrequest-preserving calculator helpers such as `calculateRunRequest`,\n`calculateReportRequest` and `calculateReport`, while reusing\ncalculator-owned `CalculatorRun*` schemas and `CalculatorServiceError`.\n\n`apps/web`\n: Native TanStack Website candidate. It owns form presentation/commands, the\n  server runner and React Atom registry. The private server binding and checked\n  public browser address reach the separate API through native RPC. The root\n  restores encoded outcomes. Tax rules and calculations remain package/API-owned;\n  the three calculator pages and bounded T003 connection are locally qualified.\n  The same app owns checked documentation page loaders, app-specific MDX\n  composition, navigation/focus, search and responsive reading. Its server\n  returns checked backend discovery documents at their conventional addresses.\n  Both Markdown representations and generated share images are locally\n  qualified. Current release documentation checking selects this app.\n  The old `apps/docs` workspace and writer workflows are retired; its\n  verified original source and dated recovery records remain addressable.\n\n`apps/docs`\n: Retirement tombstone only. The strict historical source bundle retains all\n  49 original files. The deployment runbook owns inspection and separately\n  approved recovery, rather than an active old workspace.\n\nRoot `alchemy.run.ts`\n: Static retirement record which the actual Alchemy importer refuses before\n59: *     randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n66: *   const bytes = yield* crypto.randomBytes(16)\n67: *   const uuidv4 = yield* crypto.randomUUIDv4\n95:  randomBytes(size: number): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n150:  readonly randomUUIDv4: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n155:  readonly randomUUIDv7: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\n205: * and UUID generation from `impl.randomBytes`. Digest operations delegate to\n210: * `impl.randomBytes` must return cryptographically secure bytes of the\n220: *   randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\n224: * await Effect.runPromise(testCrypto.randomBytes(4)) // => new Uint8Array([0, 0, 0, 0])\n232:    readonly randomBytes: (size: number) => Uint8Array\n239:  const randomBytesUnsafe = impl.randomBytes\n241:  const randomBytes: Crypto[\"randomBytes\"] = (size) => Effect.map(validateSize(\"randomBytes\", size), randomBytesUnsafe)\n247:  const nextDoubleUnsafe = (): number => readUint53(randomBytesUnsafe(7)) / 2 ** 53\n251:      const bytes = randomBytesUnsafe(7)\n264:    randomBytes,\n291:    randomUUIDv4: Effect.sync(() => Uuid.v4String(randomBytesUnsafe(16))),\n292:    randomUUIDv7: Effect.clockWith((clock) =>\n293:      Effect.succeed(Uuid.v7String(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(16)))\n296:      Effect.succeed(Ulid.ulidString(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(10)))\n   • Packages in scope: //\n   • Running //#test:quality-workflow:task in 1 package\n   • Remote caching disabled, using shared worktree cache\n\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: cache miss, executing e7830848b768980d\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: $ bun --bun vitest run --config tools/vitest.config.ts tools/quality-workflow\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n//:test:quality-workflow:task:  RUN  v5.0.3 /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \n"},"started_at_ms":1791367105030,"completed_at_ms":1791367105030}}
{"timestamp":"2026-10-07T09:58:25.088Z","ordinal":67893,"type":"response_item","payload":{"type":"custom_tool_call_output","id":"ctco_01a115cc-ca40-78b3-9540-b25a3c5b33f4","call_id":"call_1b9938a3ecc44a16a15cb467cf1cc6b0","output":[{"type":"input_text","text":"Script completed\nWall time 0.1 seconds\nOutput:\n"},{"type":"input_text","text":"{\"chunk_id\":\"cd5077\",\"wall_time_seconds\":0.000005541,\"exit_code\":0,\"original_token_count\":6140,\"output\":\"Warning: truncated output (original token count: 6140)\\nTotal output lines: 438\\n\\n---\\nname: package-structure\\ndescription: Scaffold, audit, migrate, or validate TypeScript packages with strict Effect v4 boundaries, explicit live-types exports, deterministic test Layers, and repository-local source conditions. Use for Effect service, RPC, or HTTP API packages; package export maps; live.layer.ts/test.layer.ts separation; package ownership; helper-sprawl prevention; or replacing stale package scaffolds. Do not use for repository-root or TanStack application scaffolding.\\n---\\n\\n# Package Structure\\n\\nBuild the narrowest package that owns a real semantic boundary. Follow the\\nrepository contract before the generic templates.\\n\\n## Resolve the repository contract\\n\\n1. Read the applicable `AGENTS.md`, root/package `README*`, architecture docs,\\n   manifests, TypeScript/lint/test config, and current package exemplars.\\n2. If invoked through a repository-local same-name skill, read its\\n   `references/repository-profile.md` first. That profile owns package manager,\\n   namespace, source condition, package exceptions, forbidden paths, and\\n   package commands.\\n3. Load the sibling [`docs-maintainer`](../docs-maintainer/SKILL.md) and its\\n   repository-local profile when present. That profile—not the package\\n   profile—owns documentation, runbook, proof, lifecycle, and archive routes.\\n4. Inspect the worktree before writes. Preserve unrelated changes and do not\\n   scaffold into a package until ownership is decided.\\n5. Load [package contract](references/package-contract.md), then only the\\n   relevant variant section in [variants](references/variants.md).\\n\\n## Select the variant\\n\\n- `effect-service`: domain/provider capability with Schemas, expected failures,\\n  a contract-only service, production Layer, and deterministic test Layer.\\n- `rpc`: RPC transport over an existing domain service. It owns RPC contracts,\\n  handlers, clients, and transport—not domain policy.\\n- `http-api`: HTTP transport over an existing domain service. It owns API\\n  groups, status mapping, handlers, browser and host-supplied clients, and routes.\\n- A transport client Layer can implement the existing domain Context.Service.\\n  Keep generated native clients private and avoid a duplicate public contract.\\n- Source-only or publishable packages are export/build policies applied after\\n  choosing the semantic variant; they are not extra module trees.\\n\\nDo not create a package for one-use code, a pass-through wrapper, a generic\\n`utils` bucket, or a boundary already owned by another package.\\n\\n## Render or audit\\n\\nFor a new package, use the canonical renderer. It refuses existing targets and\\nhas no overwrite mode. It renders the compiled-internal policy only; source-only\\nand publishable packages require a repository-specific audit/migration because\\ntheir bundler and actual publisher own the proof:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 scripts/render_package.py \\\\\\n  --kind effect-service \\\\\\n  --target /absolute/new/package \\\\\\n  --package-name @scope/catalog \\\\\\n  --source-condition @scope/source \\\\\\n  --versions /absolute/version-snapshot.json\\n```\\n\\nRPC and HTTP API also require `--domain-package @scope/catalog`. Read\\n[renderer and safety](references/renderer-and-safety.md) before invoking it.\\n\\nFor an existing package, run inspection first and make the smallest patch that\\nbrings it to the owning repository contract:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 scripts/inspect_package.py /absolute/package\\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\\n```\\n\\n## Enforce the Effect boundary\\n\\n- `schemas.ts` owns Schemas, brands, and schema-derived types.\\n- `errors.ts` owns expected failures. Serializable boundaries use the installed\\n  Schema error factory (`Schema.TaggedError` in the qualified RC); internal-only\\n  failures may use `Data.TaggedError`. Templates target the same qualified RC.\\n- `service.ts` owns only `Context.Service` contracts—never a Layer or SDK.\\n- `live.layer.ts` keeps host/SDK construction private and owns production\\n  configuration/resources.\\n- `test.layer.ts` creates deterministic substitution at the same contract and\\n  may return `{ layer, observations }` scoped to one test.\\n- Public methods use named `Effect.fn`; private Effect helpers normally use\\n  `Effect.fnUntraced`. Use `Service.of` to check implementations and the\\n  two-argument `Layer.effect` for effectful construction. Read the sibling\\n  [service and Layer recipes](../strict-effect-ts/references/services/services-and-layers.md).\\n- Decode unknown provider input/output once at ingress. Encode only at outward\\n  HTTP/RPC/provider/persistence/report boundaries.\\n- Keep operations lazy, flat, and sequential. Keep one-use mapping, decoding,\\n  and error handling beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse,\\n  independently testable policy, real I/O, or resource lifetime.\\n- Reject generic SDK callbacks, raw client access, unbranded semantic strings,\\n  primitive semantic config, runtime type-class branching, unchecked output,\\n  scattered Effect execution, and speculative helpers.\\n- When a public schema/service/transport changes, update the repository's\\n  architecture owner and affected proof/critical journeys in the same slice.\\n  Operational packages route to a repository runbook; pure packages state why\\n  no runbook applies.\\n\\nRead [Effect service rules](references/effect-service.md) before changing an\\nexternal client or live Layer.\\n\\n## Validate and hand off\\n\\nRun the repository's real format, lint, typecheck, focused tests, integration\\ntests, build, packed-artifact, and downstream commands that apply. Also run:\\n\\n```bash\\npython3 scripts/validate_package.py /absolute/package\\npython3 scripts/validate_ecosystem.py /absolute/skill-or-repository-root\\n```\\n\\nFor publishable packages, inspect the tarball produced by the repository's\\nactual publisher; `publishConfig.exports` alone is not proof that npm or Bun\\nremoved the development source condition.\\n\\nFor new or changed skill folders, run the global skill validator separately.\\nRun the local docs-maintainer checks and attach the applicable impact rows and\\nnon-claims. Report the selected variant, ownership decision, exports,\\ndocumentation/proof impact, tests, commands, and any repository-specific exception. Render receipts\\nretain official sources, qualified compatibility decisions, config digests,\\nlimitations, and non-claims. Read [maintenance](references/maintenance.md)\\nwhen updating templates, validators, or Effect APIs.\\n\\n## Strict data ownership in every package\\n\\nUse one Schema owner for each identity, constrained value and data record; infer\\nits public types and reuse its fields and record-level checks in HTTP/RPC/provider\\nand persistence codecs. Typed internal inputs/replies are already checked. Decode\\nunknown data once at its real boundary, construct newly constrained values with\\nthe owning fallible constructor, and encode only outward contracts. Meaningful\\nIDs, URLs, dates, offsets and bounded text use owning brands rather than parallel\\nprimitive definitions.\\n\\nDomain absence uses Option, including recursive source fields. Preserve old\\nformats, missing/null distinctions, literal values and source hashes in codecs\\nand compatibility tests. Use Effect Array/Record functions, immutable HashMap/\\nHashSet and exhaustive Match throughout source, tests, tools and configuration.\\nNo loops, native array traversal, reassignment or mutable/transient collection\\nmode is justified by a readonly return type. Genuine shared state belongs in an\\nowned Ref/transaction with a pure immutable update. Use readonly structured keys\\nfor Effect caches and qualify sharing, full identity and cleanup on cancellation.\\n\\nSee the sibling strict-effect-ts Schema, collection and cache owners. The generated\\npackage runs under its repository's actual strict lint configuration; the offline\\nPython shape check is not proof of TypeScript policy, execution or cloud behaviour.\\n---\\nname: effect-client-wrapper\\ndescription: Design, implement, or review a typed Effect service around a third-party SDK, API client, callback library, or Promise-based provider. Use for provider adapters, service Layers, external-client configuration, request and response Schemas, typed provider errors, retries, and test Layers. Enforce named operations, branded identifiers, Schema-backed Config, immediate output decoding, flat Effects, and the explicit rejection of generic use callbacks, raw clients, primitive config, instanceof, and unchecked SDK outputs.\\n---\\n\\n# Effect Client Wrapper\\n\\nCreate a semantic adapter owned by the application, not a generic Effect-shaped escape hatch around an SDK.\\n\\n## Establish the boundary\\n\\n1. Read applicable repository instructions, architecture docs, lint rules, installed Effect and SDK versions, and representative services and Layers.\\n2. Identify the owner of every request, response, identifier, configuration value, error, retry policy, and runtime boundary.\\n3. Research the cloned Effect v4 source, record its revision/version, then reconcile the chosen APIs with installed source/types. Use matching primary SDK source for its contract; DeepWiki through Executor MCP is secondary explanation for the upstream Effect/SDK repository.\\n4. Prefer extending an existing semantic adapter over creating a parallel client abstraction.\\n\\n## Define the public service\\n\\n- Expose named domain/provider operations such as `getCustomer`, `sendMessage`, or `renderImage`.\\n- Accept owning Schema-derived inputs and branded identifiers. Never substitute raw `id: string`.\\n- Return only decoded application-owned or explicitly provider-owned Schema types.\\n- Keep the raw SDK instance private to the live Layer or adapter module.\\n- Provide a live Layer and a mock/test Layer at the same service contract.\\n\\nDo not expose a generic `use`, `run`, `withClient`, callback, unconstrained generic result, or raw-client accessor. Those APIs erase operation ownership, output provenance, retry policy, and testability.\\n\\n## Implement each operation linearly\\n\\nUse the locally installed Effect APIs. A named operation should read as one flat program:\\n\\n1. encode the checked request at its actual outbound contract, without re-decoding internal typed data;\\n2. call exactly one SDK operation through `Effect.tryPromise`, callback adaptation, or the repository's boundary primitive;\\n3. map rejection immediately into an owner-named typed Schema error with safe diagnostics;\\n4. decode the unknown SDK result immediately with the owning output Schema;\\n5. apply operation-specific retry, a complete-operation deadline, tracing, metrics, and redaction policy;\\n6. return the decoded value without another wrapper layer.\\n\\nUse `Effect.gen`, meaningful `Effect.fn`, or local equivalents for readable sequential work. Keep one-use request mapping, rejection mapping, and decoding beside the operation. Extract only demonstrated reuse, stable domain policy, or a real I/O boundary.\\n\\nFor native Effect HTTP/RPC clients, reuse the domain service contract instead\\nof exposing the generated client or inventing a second public service. Keep\\nSchema-selected response checks and named transport/decode failures with their\\nsemantic owner. Promise adaptation is unnecessary when the client already\\nreturns Effect.\\n\\nA deadline includes headers and decoded body. Test both stalled phases, one\\ntotal budget after late headers, underlying cleanup and earlier interruption.\\nDo not reset the clock at each phase or add an automatic retry by default.\\n\\n## Configure safely\\n\\n- Model semantic configuration with Schema and `Config`/`ConfigProvider`, using the repository's supported API.\\n- Give configuration an owner-named structure rather than passing independent primitive strings and numbers through the codebase.\\n- Keep secrets redacted and unwrap them only for immediate SDK construction.\\n- Construct the SDK once in the live Layer unless its documented lifecycle requires scoped acquisition and release.\\n- O…240 tokens truncated… operation. Keep safe refusal details.\\n\\nScope callback execution and registration together. Release unregisters or\\naborts registration and interrupts unfinished callbacks. Accommodate synchronous\\nand Promise return forms only where a supported host requires them. Confine the\\nbridge and justified lint exceptions to the named adapter. For a browser tool\\ninterface, follow the sibling\\n[MCP/WebMCP pattern](../strict-effect-ts/references/applications/mcp-and-webmcp.md).\\n\\nFor a PostHog browser SDK in a React-owned Effect Atom runtime, read the\\n[PostHog browser adapter guide](references/posthog-browser.md). It covers scoped\\nSDK ownership, explicit event operations, the synchronous capture callback,\\nprivacy controls and real-client delivery proof. Native Distilled management\\nand backend Effect HTTP capture do not need Promise bridges.\\n\\n## Test and enforce\\n\\nTest service operations through mock/test Layers and test the live adapter boundary narrowly. Cover success, request encoding, provider rejection mapping, output decode failure, redaction, timeout/retry policy, and non-idempotent retry exclusion.\\n\\nUpdate affected architecture docs, READMEs, lint/custom rules and fixtures, CI commands, SPEC/tasks, and repository-owned skills. Add the narrowest reliable static or stale-pattern check the repository supports.\\n\\nAcceptance requires zero examples or public APIs containing:\\n\\n- a generic SDK `use` callback or raw-client accessor;\\n- a raw `id: string` where an owning branded ID exists;\\n- primitive/manual semantic configuration in place of Schema-backed `Config`;\\n- `instanceof` provider-error branching;\\n- an SDK result escaping without immediate Schema decoding;\\n- a one-use wrapper/helper that merely forwards, maps, reads one property, or hides the SDK call.\\n\\nRun the repository's actual format, lint, typecheck, test, build, boundary, skill-validation, and stale-pattern checks appropriate to the change. Do not invent commands or claim the wrapper is safe when any prohibited pattern remains.\\n\\n## Keep checked values checked\\n\\nThe live adapter owns actual untrusted replies. Decode there once and return the\\nowning inferred type. A caller does not re-decode a typed service reply; a test\\nLayer follows that same contract. Reuse the domain's branded fields and shared\\nwhole-record checks in a provider codec, including checks lost when fields are\\nspread. Keep expected identity, completeness and range checks as domain policy.\\n\\nNew branded values use the installed fallible constructor; map its issue to the\\noperation's named error. Plain mappings from checked values stay immutable with\\nEffect Array/Record functions. Optional provider/source fields decode to Option;\\nhistorical missing/null distinctions belong in the source codec. Internal caches\\nuse checked readonly keys directly, with equality, full-address and cancellation\\ntests. Neither SDK typings nor another internal decoder replaces boundary proof.\\n---\\ndocument_type: architecture\\nlifecycle: current\\nauthority: canonical\\nowner: taxkit-architecture-owner\\nlast_reviewed: 2026-10-07\\nreview_trigger: package, app, root composition, or semantic ownership change\\n---\\n\\n# Package ownership\\n\\nPackage ownership defines where new TaxKit code should belong as the repo\\ngrows and which package will be allowed to define canonical contracts. It does\\nnot imply that every named package exists today.\\n\\n## Scope\\n\\nThis doc routes ownership decisions. [Package boundaries](./package-boundaries.md)\\ncontains the proposed package map and dependency direction.\\n\\nCurrent implemented code lives in:\\n\\n- `apps/api`\\n- `apps/web`\\n- `packages/core`\\n- `packages/calculators`\\n- `packages/content`\\n- `packages/docs-content`\\n- `packages/docs-examples`\\n- `packages/docs-fumadocs`\\n- `packages/api/http`\\n- `packages/api/rpc`\\n: Private compiled native Effect RPC transport. It owns separately versioned\\n  calculator and documentation procedures, thin named service handlers, checked POST/JSON ingress and\\n  caller-scoped private client Layers. Calculator/content Schemas remain with\\n  their existing owners. Explicit test-only composition is separate; no app\\n  runtime or backend fallback belongs here. T003 app-host composition is locally accepted; T009 exported tracing remains open.\\n\\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\\n- `packages/rules/au/income-tax`\\n- `packages/rules/au/pay`\\n- `packages/rules/au/stsl`\\n- `packages/scripts`\\n- `packages/testing`\\n- `packages/tsconfig`\\n\\nCurrent planned ownership placeholder:\\n\\n- `packages/ui`\\n\\nThe placeholder directories contain README guidance only. Do not route runtime\\nimports, source ownership or build expectations to them until package manifests\\nand source exports exist.\\n\\n## Main areas\\n\\n`packages/core`\\n: Implemented shared primitives, fact descriptors, rule descriptors, graph\\nmetadata, trace and ledger contracts, common tagged errors and calculation\\nengine service. Public trace inputs and results are JSON values, and\\n`SourceExtract.rowContract` names the extracted row contract without using the\\nvague former `shape` field.\\n\\n`packages/domain/au/*`\\n: Planned Australian date dimensions and domain facts that are not owned by a\\nsingle rule pack.\\n\\n`packages/rules/au/*`\\n: Official Australian rule packs, parameter tables, algorithms, source\\nreferences, graph metadata, rule-owned calculator ids/context literals and\\ngolden tests. Current implemented packages are `pay`, `income-tax` and `stsl`.\\n\\n`packages/testing`\\n: Shared test helpers for workspace packages. It must not become a back door\\nfor production-only runtime helpers.\\n\\n`packages/scripts`\\n: Implemented private repository-automation package. It owns schema-backed,\\n  Effect-native orchestration that crosses package owners, including the\\n  release-readiness command, its command-runner service, live process layer,\\n  deterministic test layer and runtime entrypoint. It invokes canonical root\\n  and package commands without moving or duplicating their validator logic.\\n  Constructor functions use `create*`; deprecated `make*` aliases remain only\\n  for package compatibility.\\n\\n`tools/repository-paths`\\n: Root-owned repository portability gate. It owns the closed machine-local path\\n  policy, schema-backed safe findings, typed local process and file failures,\\n  focused tests and Bun runtime entrypoint. It is not a reusable file-scanner\\n  package. `@taxkit/scripts` may invoke its root command but must not mirror the\\n  policy.\\n\\n`packages/api/http`\\n: Implemented HTTP API package. It owns Effect HTTP API definitions, boundary\\nschemas, thin server handlers, OpenAPI, typed HTTP clients and HTTP\\nstatus/transport annotations. Its narrow `request-boundary` export also owns the\\nshared streamed HTTP-body admission used by the RPC and native app hosts. The\\nRPC package depends inward on that export and retains its compatibility alias;\\nthe HTTP package has no RPC runtime dependency. Client and Layer constructors use `create*`;\\ndeprecated `make*` aliases remain at the export owner for compatibility.\\n\\n`packages/calculators`\\n: Implemented reusable calculator orchestration package. It owns calculator\\ncatalog composition schemas, calculator service methods, metadata projections,\\ngraph response construction, schema-guided error shaping and\\nrule-pack/scenario composition used by HTTP, SDK, CLI and in-process callers.\\nIt also owns the canonical reusable calculator run schemas named\\n`CalculatorRun*` and the `CalculatorServiceError` union. HTTP-only public\\nenvelopes and status annotations stay in `packages/api/http`; SDK schema\\nexports may re-export calculator-owned run contracts but must not duplicate\\nthem. Service interfaces use the precise `Contract` suffix rather than the\\nvague `Shape` suffix. It depends on `packages/core` and rule packages, but it\\nmust not depend on HTTP handlers, SDK clients, CLI commands or app runtime\\nmodules.\\n\\n`packages/sdk/typescript`\\n: Implemented private TypeScript SDK package for a future public SDK\\npublication. It owns browser-safe schemas, typed calculation facades,\\nEffect-native subpaths, jurisdiction modules, examples, compatibility tests and\\npacked-artifact checks. Its strict downstream validator may orchestrate the\\nnine-package release closure and materialize package-declared publication\\nexports in temporary tarballs, but package manifests and exports remain owned\\nby their packages. It must not depend on `@taxkit/api-http`; HTTP\\ntransports call the calculator service directly; the SDK is a test-only\\ncomparison dependency of the HTTP package. Its Effect entrypoint owns\\nrequest-preserving calculator helpers such as `calculateRunRequest`,\\n`calculateReportRequest` and `calculateReport`, while reusing\\ncalculator-owned `CalculatorRun*` schemas and `CalculatorServiceError`.\\n\\n`apps/web`\\n: Native TanStack Website candidate. It owns form presentation/commands, the\\n  server runner and React Atom registry. The private server binding and checked\\n  public browser address reach the separate API through native RPC. The root\\n  restores encoded outcomes. Tax rules and calculations remain package/API-owned;\\n  the three calculator pages and bounded T003 connection are locally qualified.\\n  The same app owns checked documentation page loaders, app-specific MDX\\n  composition, navigation/focus, search and responsive reading. Its server\\n  returns checked backend discovery documents at their conventional addresses.\\n  Both Markdown representations and generated share images are locally\\n  qualified. Current release documentation checking selects this app.\\n  The old `apps/docs` workspace and writer workflows are retired; its\\n  verified original source and dated recovery records remain addressable.\\n\\n`apps/docs`\\n: Retirement tombstone only. The strict historical source bundle retains all\\n  49 original files. The deployment runbook owns inspection and separately\\n  approved recovery, rather than an active old workspace.\\n\\nRoot `alchemy.run.ts`\\n: Static retirement record which the actual Alchemy importer refuses before\\n59: *     randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\\n66: *   const bytes = yield* crypto.randomBytes(16)\\n67: *   const uuidv4 = yield* crypto.randomUUIDv4\\n95:  randomBytes(size: number): Effect.Effect<Uint8Array, PlatformError.PlatformError>\\n150:  readonly randomUUIDv4: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\\n155:  readonly randomUUIDv7: Effect.Effect<string, PlatformError.PlatformError>\\n205: * and UUID generation from `impl.randomBytes`. Digest operations delegate to\\n210: * `impl.randomBytes` must return cryptographically secure bytes of the\\n220: *   randomBytes: (size) => new Uint8Array(size),\\n224: * await Effect.runPromise(testCrypto.randomBytes(4)) // => new Uint8Array([0, 0, 0, 0])\\n232:    readonly randomBytes: (size: number) => Uint8Array\\n239:  const randomBytesUnsafe = impl.randomBytes\\n241:  const randomBytes: Crypto[\\\"randomBytes\\\"] = (size) => Effect.map(validateSize(\\\"randomBytes\\\", size), randomBytesUnsafe)\\n247:  const nextDoubleUnsafe = (): number => readUint53(randomBytesUnsafe(7)) / 2 ** 53\\n251:      const bytes = randomBytesUnsafe(7)\\n264:    randomBytes,\\n291:    randomUUIDv4: Effect.sync(() => Uuid.v4String(randomBytesUnsafe(16))),\\n292:    randomUUIDv7: Effect.clockWith((clock) =>\\n293:      Effect.succeed(Uuid.v7String(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(16)))\\n296:      Effect.succeed(Ulid.ulidString(clock.currentTimeMillisUnsafe(), randomBytesUnsafe(10)))\\n   • Packages in scope: //\\n   • Running //#test:quality-workflow:task in 1 package\\n   • Remote caching disabled, using shared worktree cache\\n\\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: cache miss, executing e7830848b768980d\\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: $ bun --bun vitest run --config tools/vitest.config.ts tools/quality-workflow\\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \\n//:test:quality-workflow:task:  RUN  v5.0.3 /Users/cooper/.codex/worktrees/aac7/taxkit\\n//:test:quality-workflow:task: \\n\"}"}],"internal_chat_message_metadata_passthrough":{"turn_id":"01a11571-1a58-7282-903b-860cd96057b2","create_time":1791367105.08815}},"metadata":{"client_authored":false,"fallback_token_limit_override":12000}}
